title: A switch for signals
mode: vhdl
chapter: Choosing
check: behaves
---- intro
A **multiplexer** chooses: a select input `s` decides which of two inputs goes through to `y`. When `s` is 0, `y` is `a`; when `s` is 1, `y` is `b`. It is how a circuit picks between results.

Built from gates:

- "`a` while `s` is 0" is `a and not s`
- "`b` while `s` is 1" is `b and s`

Only one of them can be 1 at a time, so an OR joins them.
---- task
Write the multiplexer with gates: `y` follows `a` when `s` is 0, and `b` when `s` is 1.
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity mux_gates is
  port(
    a : in  std_logic;
    b : in  std_logic;
    s : in  std_logic;
    y : out std_logic
  );
end mux_gates;

architecture rtl of mux_gates is
begin
    -- write it here
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity mux_gates is
  port(
    a : in  std_logic;
    b : in  std_logic;
    s : in  std_logic;
    y : out std_logic
  );
end mux_gates;

architecture rtl of mux_gates is
begin
    y <= (a and not s) or (b and s);
end rtl;
---- hint1
Two ANDs in brackets, joined by an OR. One of them uses `not s`.
---- hint2
`y <= (a and not s) or (b and s);`
---- solution
`y <= (a and not s) or (b and s);` The first bracket lets `a` through while `s` is 0, the second lets `b` through while `s` is 1.
---- done
Four gates to choose. There is a much shorter way to say it in VHDL, as you will see next.
